<GuidePage
  canonicalPath="/guides/how-to-calculate-quilt-fabric/"
  index={false}
  title="How to Calculate Fabric Yardage for a Quilt"
  description="A practical piece-list method for converting quilt dimensions into usable-width rows, safety allowance, purchase yardage, and a cutting plan."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Start with pieces, not quilt area</h2>
    <p>
      Quilt yardage depends on how shapes fit across the usable fabric width.
      Multiplying the quilt’s finished area by a percentage can miss seam
      allowance, repeated pieces, directional constraints, and partial rows. A
      dependable estimate begins with a piece list grouped by fabric.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>List each rectangular piece, its quantity, and its fabric.</li>
      <li>Convert finished measurements to cut measurements once.</li>
      <li>Measure or choose a conservative usable width after selvages.</li>
      <li>
        Decide whether each piece may rotate and whether the print is
        directional.
      </li>
      <li>
        Arrange whole pieces across WOF and round the number of rows upward.
      </li>
      <li>Add a stated safety allowance and round the purchase upward.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>A repeated-rectangle example</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose one fabric needs 24 rectangles cut at 3½ × 6½ inches and has 40
      inches of usable width. When rotation is allowed, six pieces fit across
      with their 6½-inch edge across WOF. Four rows at 3½ inches each consume 14
      inches down the bolt. A 5% allowance makes 14.7 inches, which rounds up to
      the next eighth-yard increment: 18 inches, or ½ yard.
    </p>
    <p>
      The{' '}<a href="/calculators/fabric-yardage/"
        >fabric yardage calculator</a
      >{' '}performs this repeated-piece calculation and shows the chosen
      orientation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://help.prequilt.com/prequilt-101/fabric-calculator/calculations-explained/fabric-cutting-diagrams/"
        >PreQuilt's calculation explanation</a
      >{' '}
      is a useful comparison for why actual row geometry matters.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>When the quilt uses several piece sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Treat each fabric independently and account for every piece exactly once.
      A practical plan can fill space left in a strip row with smaller pieces,
      but it must keep pieces inside usable width and respect orientation. This
      is a layout problem, so the best estimate comes from actual placements
      rather than a second area-only formula.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before calculating, review{' '}<a href="/guides/finished-vs-cut-size/"
        >finished versus cut size</a
      >{' '}and{' '}<a href="/guides/width-of-fabric/">usable width of fabric</a
      >. Then enter all fabrics and pieces in the planner below; it returns a
      shopping total, cutting list, and diagram from the same deterministic
      layout.
    </p>
  </section>
